# Lesson 8: Editing power tools

Lesson 3 covered motions and text objects. This one adds the tools that make
everyday edits shorter: wrapping things in quotes and brackets, more text
objects, splitting and joining lists, find and replace across the project,
editing folders as text, and getting back anything you've undone.

The exercise lines in this lesson are checked: ✗ until the line is right, ✓
after. Mess one up? `u`{normal} undoes.

# 8.1 Surround: add, change, delete

  `ys`{normal}{motion}{char}   add          `ysiw"`{normal} wraps the word in "quotes"
  `yss`{normal}{char}          add around the whole line
  `cs`{normal}{old}{new}       change       `cs'"`{normal} turns 'this' into "this"
  `ds`{normal}{char}           delete       `ds(`{normal} removes the brackets round the cursor
  `gs`{normal}{char}           in visual mode, wrap the selection

The cursor can be anywhere inside. For {char}, `q`{normal} means "whichever quotes" and
`b`{normal} "whichever brackets", and `t`{normal} is an HTML/JSX tag (it asks for the name).
`(`{normal} adds spaces inside, `)`{normal} doesn't: `ysiw)`{normal} gives (word).

Put quotes round alice (cursor on alice, `ysiw"`{normal}):
{{const name = alice; ||| const name = "alice";}}

Make the single quotes double (cursor inside them, `cs'"`{normal}):
{{import { addMoney } from './money'; ||| import { addMoney } from "./money";}}

Drop the needless brackets (cursor inside them, `ds(`{normal}):
{{const n = (count); ||| const n = count;}}

Turn the brackets into square ones (`csb]`{normal}):
{{const ids = (1, 2, 3); ||| const ids = [1, 2, 3];}}

# 8.2 More text objects

Lesson 3's `i"`{normal} and `i(`{normal} need you to say WHICH quote or bracket. These don't:

  `iq`{normal} / `aq`{normal}   inside / around the nearest quotes: "", '' or ``
  `ib`{normal} / `ab`{normal}   inside / around the nearest brackets: (), [] or {}

And the Treesitter ones, which understand the code:

  `if`{normal} / `af`{normal}   inside / around the function you're in
  `ia`{normal} / `aa`{normal}   inside / around an argument (`aa`{normal} takes the comma too)
  `ik`{normal} / `ak`{normal}   inside / around a block

Add `n`{normal} for the NEXT one and `l`{normal} for the LAST (previous) one, so you don't even
need to move there first: `cinq`{normal} changes inside the next quotes.

Change the text in the backticks to hi (`ciq`{normal} with the cursor inside):
{{greet(`good morning`) ||| greet(`hi`)}}

From the START of the line, change "old" to "new" in one go (`0`{normal} then `cinq`{normal}):
{{const label = "old"; ||| const label = "new";}}

Delete the middle argument and its comma (cursor on 2, `daa`{normal}):
{{price(a, 2, "GBP") ||| price(a, "GBP")}}

`daa`{normal}, `cia`{normal} and `vaf`{normal} are worth making habits in TypeScript: reorder or
remove arguments, or select a whole function to send to Claude (`<Space>As`{normal}).

# 8.3 Split and join

  `<Space>m`{normal}   toggle between one line and one item per line, for the object,
              array, argument list or JSX props under the cursor (treesj)

Try it on this line, twice (there's nothing to check here):

    const item = { sku: "TEA-1", qty: 2, price: { pence: 350, currency: "GBP" } };

With the cursor on `sku` it splits the outer object; on `pence` only the inner
one. It works the same in Rust, Python, Go, Lua and JSON.

# 8.4 Find and replace across the project

  `<Space>ss`{normal}   search and replace in the whole project (grug-far)
  `<Space>sw`{normal}   the same, starting with the word under the cursor
  `<Space>sf`{normal}   only in the current file

It opens a buffer with Search, Replace and Files fields at the top; results
appear live as you type, with the change previewed. Normal Vim editing works
in every field. In that buffer (`,`{normal} is your local leader):

  `,r`{normal}  replace everywhere   `g?`{normal}  all its keys   `,c`{normal}  close
  edit a result line and `,s`{normal} writes just those edits back to the files

 1. `:Practice`{vim}, then on `formatMoney` in src/index.ts press `<Space>sw`{normal}.
 2. In Replace, type formatPrice. Look at the preview: index.ts and money.ts.
 3. `,r`{normal} to do it, then `,c`{normal}. Language-server rename (`<Space>lr`{normal}, lesson 5) is
    better for a symbol; grug-far is for text, comments, strings, configs and
    regexes (it's ripgrep underneath; try `--fixed-strings` in the Flags field
    when the search has special characters).

# 8.5 Edit a folder like a text file

  `<Space>O`{normal}   open the current file's folder in Oil
  `-`{normal}          (in Oil) go up a folder     `<CR>`{normal} open a file or folder
  `g?`{normal}         all of Oil's keys

Each line is a file. Rename one by editing its line, delete with `dd`{normal}, move
with `dd`{normal} and `p`{normal} in another folder, create with `o`{normal} and a name (end it with /
for a folder). Nothing happens until `:w`{vim}, which shows what it will do and
asks to confirm.

 1. In the practice project, `<Space>O`{normal} from src/cart.ts.
 2. Rename money.ts to currency.ts by editing the line (`ciw`{normal} on money), `:w`{vim}, `y`.
    The language server updates the imports (check index.ts).
 3. `u`{normal} works in Oil too: rename it back the same way.

The explorer (`<Space>e`{normal}) is still best for seeing the tree; Oil is best for
doing several file operations at once.

# 8.6 The undo tree

Vim never throws undo history away: if you undo, then type something new, the
old changes are on another branch, not gone. `<Space>fu`{normal} shows the tree.

  `<Space>fu`{normal}  open the undo tree    `j`{normal}/`k`{normal} move through it, with a diff below
  `<CR>`{normal}       go back to that state  `q`{normal} close

 1. Change this line to "first", undo, then change it to "second" (both with `ciw`{normal}):

    word

 2. Plain `u`{normal} can't get "first" back now. `<Space>fu`{normal} can: pick that state.

# 8.7 Small helpers you'll just notice

  TypeScript errors are rewritten in plain English (ts-error-translator); hover
  them with `gl`{normal} as usual.
  Markdown shows headings, tables, checkboxes and code formatted in the buffer;
  the raw text appears on the line you're editing.
  SQL files: `:SqlsExecuteQuery`{vim} runs the query under the cursor once a
  connection is set up (sqls config in the project).

# Recap

  surround   `ysiw"`  `cs'"`  `ds(`  `csb]`  visual `gs`
  objects    `iq` `ib`  `if` `ia` `ik`   next `in…`  last `il…`
  split/join `<Space>m`    find/replace `<Space>ss` `<Space>sw`
  Oil `<Space>O`    undo tree `<Space>fu`

Search any of these with navi (`Ctrl-G` in the shell): try "surround".
